INT. LITTLE LEAGUE DUGOUT - RAIN - DUSK

A concrete bunker with a plank bench. Rain ropes off the tin roof. The scoreboard outside reads 14 to 2, bottom of the sixth. Wet jerseys. Silence.

COACH ROSCOE FINN, 60, hunched under a ball cap, studies his clipboard as if it will change the score. PIA, 11, the catcher, picks at a strap on her shin guard. LEVI, 10, stares at his glove. TAMSIN, 12, the shortstop, kicks at a puddle forming at her cleats. OTIS, 9, the smallest, sits at the end of the bench with a bat across his lap, his mouth working with a sentence he hasn't dared to say.

ROSCOE
(to the whole dugout)
All right, listen up. One more inning. Doesn't matter what the board says.

Nobody looks up. Rain pounds.

ROSCOE (CONT'D)
(to Tamsin)
Tams, you've got a good arm. Use it.

TAMSIN
Yes, Coach.

ROSCOE
(to himself, under his breath)
Fourteen to two. Lord.

OTIS
Coach?

ROSCOE
Yeah, Otis.

OTIS
Can we just play it out?

Roscoe looks at him for a long moment. The kids go still. He lowers the clipboard onto the bench.

ROSCOE
Yeah, bud. We can do that.

He stands, slaps the roof of the dugout twice with the flat of his hand. Pia puts on her mask. Levi pulls his glove on. Tamsin lifts her head. Otis grips the bat, stands, and walks out into the rain.
